# LeakSanitizer suppressions for oans (ASAN builds: make SANITIZE=address...).
#
# Point LSAN at this file with LSAN_OPTIONS=suppressions=tests/lsan.supp; the
# Makefile's sanitizer run does it automatically for both `test` and
# `integration`. It is the ASAN analogue of tests/valgrind.supp.
#
# GLib caches idle worker threads from its thread pools (oans's walker/dedupe
# pools and the progress thread) rather than joining them all at exit, so the
# per-thread bookkeeping and TLS GLib allocates for them can still be live when
# LSAN scans. That is what these three are for, and they are named by
# *function* on purpose.
#
# **Never widen this to `leak:libglib-2.0`.** It was that for a long time, and
# a module-wide pattern matches any leak whose stack passes through GLib - which
# is nearly all of oans's heap, since it holds everything in GPtrArray,
# GHashTable, GRegex and GString. Measured: delete one `g_free()` from
# glob_set_free() and the suite leaks 769,200 bytes in 32,050 allocations,
# LeakSanitizer names glob_set_new() as the site, and the run still exits 0. A
# mutation sweep of src/glob.c under SANITIZE=address found 66 survivors with
# that pattern and 54 without it, all 13 of the difference a deleted free, unref
# or string-free. A suppression that broad turns the ASAN leg into a slower copy
# of the plain one.
#
# The three below did not fire at all when this was measured (GLib 2.80,
# `LSAN_OPTIONS=print_suppressions=1` over a scan and a dedupe: no suppressions
# used, no leaks). They are kept as insurance against a GLib that caches more
# eagerly rather than as a known requirement - so if one of them starts
# matching, that is worth looking at rather than assuming.
leak:g_thread_proxy
leak:g_private_get
leak:g_thread_self
